            "Start with what 'good' means. A request such as 'accurate, fast and safe' cannot be tested, so the first task is to turn it into success criteria that are specific, measurable, achievable and relevant, across several dimensions at once. For claims triage that might be: at least 92 percent correct routing on 1,000 held-out claims, 95th-percentile response time under four seconds, cost under two cents per claim, and fewer than one output in a thousand flagged unsafe. Even hazy goals such as safety can be quantified. The dimensions trade off: a prompt that lifts accuracy but doubles latency and cost is not an improvement unless the other targets still hold.\n\n"
            "Next comes the evaluation dataset: cases, each with an input and a reference answer or a grading rule. It should mirror the real task distribution, so sample anonymized production traffic and add deliberate hard cases such as missing fields, ambiguous requests, other languages and adversarial text. Anthropic favors volume with automated grading over a few perfect hand-graded cases. Keep a held-out test set that is never used while writing prompts, choosing examples or tuning rules. If failed evaluation cases are pasted into the prompt as few-shot examples, the test has leaked into the prompt: the score reads 98 percent while production delivers 86.\n\n"
            "Grade with the cheapest method that is reliable. Code-based grading (exact match, schema validation, unit tests) is fast, deterministic and scales to every change, so use it wherever the answer is checkable. Human graders are the most flexible but slow and expensive; keep them for calibration and hard cases. For judgments that rules cannot make, such as tone, use a model grader with a written rubric of explicit, independently checkable criteria, a constrained verdict (a label or a 1-5 scale) and room to reason before it scores. Then calibrate it against human labels on a sample, and fix the rubric if agreement is low. Expect judge bias, such as favoring longer answers or the one listed first, and counter it by randomizing order.\n\n"
            "Evaluation is a loop. Run a baseline, read a sample of failures and sort them by root cause: a retrieval miss, an ambiguous instruction, a skipped step, a wrong tool or truncation. Error analysis shows which fix removes the most failures; a step that must always happen belongs in code, not in a sentence the model may skip. Change one thing at a time and rerun. Keep a regression suite, a fixed versioned set that grows with every production incident and runs on each prompt, model, tool or retrieval change. Outputs vary from run to run, so repeat runs and compare intervals rather than single scores.\n\n"
            "Offline results only show that a change is safe to try, so release in stages. Shadow mode runs the candidate on live inputs without showing or acting on its output. A canary exposes a small, watched slice of traffic and widens only while quality, latency, cost and safety hold. An A/B test randomly and consistently assigns concurrent users to the current and candidate versions, with the primary metric, guardrail metrics and sample size fixed beforehand. Comparing this week with last week, or stopping when one arm leads, are classic flawed designs, and a variant that breaks a guardrail does not ship as is."
