            "Optimization starts with measurement, not tricks. Compare configurations on cost per completed task rather than price per token: a stronger model that finishes in fewer turns, or a cheaper model that fails often and needs retries, can reverse a price-list ranking, and failed attempts still bill their tokens. List the candidates (model tier, effort level, prompt, retrieval depth, caching), run each on your own evaluation set and plot pass rate against cost per task. The configurations that nothing beats on both axes form the cost-quality frontier; choose the cheapest one that clears your quality bar, and price the hardest tenth of tasks too, because the tail carries a large share of spend.\n\n"
            "The biggest free lever is prompt caching. An agent resends its whole growing conversation on every turn, so cost climbs roughly with the square of the turn count, and caching the stable prefix makes each resend far cheaper. A cache write costs a premium over normal input (1.25 times for the five-minute lifetime, 2 times for the one-hour lifetime) and a read costs a small fraction of it (a tenth on most models, less on some). So a five-minute cache repays itself after one reuse and a one-hour cache after two. Keep the prefix identical, put per-request text last, and watch the share of input read from cache: a healthy loop reads well over 80 percent, and a sudden drop means the prefix changed. Cache reads also stay out of the input-token rate limit on most models.\n\n"
